00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:21.770 align:start position:0%
chaos and scale have always been the  foundation of the Battlefield franchise  and battlefield 5 is no different squads  of soldiers relentlessly push towards  objectives with either shear force or  improvised tactics while gunfire and  explosions ring throughout the beautiful  but war-torn landscapes it's an  overwhelming sensory experience and for  the most part a fine execution of a  familiar formula  battlefield 5 goes back to World War 2  zero peon theater as a backdrop for  first-person shooting and vehicular  combat in large multiplayer matches not  too dissimilar from battlefield one  where every weapon has a distinct weight  and impact that comes through vividly in  both sight and sound the core of  battlefield remains mostly untouched but  small tweaks are welcome additions  ground troops are even more deadly this  time around with a revamped ballistics  model that results in reduced time to  kill for skilled players all players  regardless of class can revive squad  mates which highly encouraged is  sticking together but it doesn't negate  the importance of the medic classes  instant revive class roles and teamwork  are further emphasized by the attrition  system the change is made to resource  scarcity the support classes ammo  distribution comes in clutch when you  survive multiple firefights while the  assault class has a perk that grants  more ammo upon scavenging the game's  health system is partially auto  regenerating but relies on having a med  kit on hand distributed by medics as  impactful as attrition sounds it's not  so overbearing as to drastically  shake-up battlefield score though it  does make going rogue less viable  the fortification system lets you build  predetermined structures like sandbag  walls barbed wire coils and Czech  hedgehogs there are no resources tied to  this though the support class builds  much faster than others and could prop  up things like stationary guns overall  it feels a bit tacked on but there's no  denying its effectiveness in the right  situation sandbags for a little cover  can go a long way for holding down an  objective when everything else has been  reduced to rubble above all else  battlefield 5 truly shines in Grand  Operations a series of three consecutive  matches or rounds intertwine by brief  narrative bits inspired by world war two  events the dress-up is a nice touch but  the real reason Grand Operations works  is because it keeps up the momentum from  round to round and packages focused  well-executed modes like airborne  breakthrough in frontlines frontlines in  particular plays out like a tug of war  where teams fight over different  objectives in sequential order within  defined sections of a map depending on  the phase of the match this game type  isn't entirely new however the mechanics  built around battlefield 5 along with  the shift in map dynamics in each phase  makes the game an absolute thrill to  play it naturally ushers a team's  attention to a handful of clear  objectives and provides a method to the  madness  creating a satisfying push and pull  where success feels earned unfortunately  the series staple of conquest has become  the weakest link which has devolved into  a match long carousel a flag captures  easy kills and cheap deaths most maps  feel too condensed and disjointed for  the high player count and mechanics of  conquest you're often caught from behind  or flanked by enemies that simply  stumble upon you it goes both ways as  you'll often find enemies squads with  their backs turn because you lucked into  a certain spawn and ran in the right  direction there are a few bugs in  battlefield 5 some that are forgivable  like wild ragdoll physics but some are  more problematic rarely would you have  to revive a squad made by a door but  you're likely to only get the prompt to  interact with the door instead of  reviving on very rare occasions the map  goes blank when trying to enlarge it or  health facts just don't work thankfully  it's not enough to overshadow the game's  best parts all the while you'll be  earning xp for a number of separate  progression paths there isn't a ton to  unlock but levelling up weapon  proficiencies  to customize them to your play style and  various weapons and pieces of equipment  unlock as you rank up classes it's a  fairly sensible system though the same  can't be said about vehicles since  they're hard to come by and each one  ranks up separately customizing each  class's appearance is where Cosmetics  like different uniforms and weapon skins  come into play it's also how company  coins factor in the in-game currency  that you earn through challenges like  daily orders or assignments or finishing  matches most cosmetics can be acquired  with company coins but you should note  that weapon and vehicle perks are also  types of company coins at least a  relatively low cost there are no micro  transactions at the moment but are said  to be implemented in the future for  cosmetics only battlefield 5 isn't  solely a multiplayer endeavor with the  return of war stories the single-player  campaign it attempts to present a more  earnest tone to a brutal conflict and  highlight lesser-known parts of world  war two like the Norwegian resistance  and the Senegalese Ti lowers who fought  for the French army amid racial  discrimination the effort is admirable  especially when it comes to the tier I  lower campaign the mix of stealth in  combat and Nord Lee's as a one-person  army can be fun and showcases some of  the game's best set pieces but the  English side is an eye rolling series of  tedious missions that goes for a more  lighthearted tone that doesn't work war  stories has its moments but it's all  over the place in terms of design tone  and style currently battlefield 5 still  has features to implement as part of its  game as a service approach designated  tides of war  it's an exciting prospect that there's a  lot more to come at no additional cost  but you can't help but feel that the  launch package could have been a bit  more dense and unfortunately we won't be  able to evaluate those parts of the game  until they arrive the series has a  winning formula that battlefield 5  doesn't deviate far from at least for  now conquest and the map roster don't  mesh well together  however grand operations and the other  modes within it steal the show and  create some of the greatest moments the  franchise is offered you might be  surprised by the impact of the slight  changes made for this game especially  when you're deep into pushing objectives  and frontlines alongside teammates  fulfilling their roles  that's when battlefield 5 is at its best
 

